insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a7480a40-273e-5392-8be8-2cd9e5ea0691', 'rg3-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Drums Around the World (a Web Page)$q$, $q$This is the text of a page from a children's music website. At the top of the page is a search box. Below it is a menu with three links: Drum Shapes, How Drums Make Sound, and Make Your Own Drum.

Heading: How Drums Make Sound

When you hit a drum, the top of the drum shakes very fast. This shaking is called vibration. The vibration moves the air around the drum. Then the moving air reaches your ears, and you hear a sound. A tight drum top shakes faster, so it makes a higher sound. A loose drum top makes a lower sound.

Heading: Drum Shapes

Drums come in many shapes. A tall, narrow drum is often played with the hands. A wide, flat frame drum can be held in one hand. Some drums have two sides, and players hit both.

Heading: Make Your Own Drum

You can make a drum at home. Stretch a balloon over an empty can. Tape it tightly. Tap it with a pencil and listen.

Picture caption: A girl named Amara holds a frame drum at a school music night.

Sidebar, Listen Closely: Press gently on the middle of a drum while you tap it. The sound will get quieter because the top cannot vibrate as much.$q$, 'draft')
