   $q$Why does the author tell us that a few notes still wobbled when Kai played for Grandpa?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To show that Kai does not have to be perfect, because getting better happens one step at a time.","is_correct":true},{"text":"To show that Grandpa did not like the song.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"To show that Kai never practiced before Sunday.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"To show that the harmonica was broken from the first try.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To show that Kai does not have to be perfect, because getting better happens one step at a time.$q$, $q$Grandpa says, "Every wobble is a step forward," so the wobbly notes show that learning keeps going even after the song works. Kai practiced "every afternoon," and Grandpa "clapped and clapped," so Kai did practice and Grandpa did like it.$q$, 'draft', 'b51b3bc0-42c2-5985-b621-f0648f8f9fc4')
