-- Passage rg4-argument-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f4ef9bd8-0f01-504e-9f38-09b1906450fb', 'rg4-argument-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Towns Grew Beside Rivers$q$, $q$Long ago, people often built their towns right beside rivers. This was not an accident. Rivers gave early towns many of the things they needed to grow.

First, rivers gave people water. Families needed water for drinking, cooking and washing. Farmers needed water for their crops. Carrying water a long way was hard work, so living close to a river saved time.

Second, rivers were like roads. Before trains and trucks, it was hard to move heavy things over land. One boat could carry as much as many wagons. Farmers could float grain and wood down the river to sell in other towns.

Third, rivers gave towns power. Builders put water wheels beside fast rivers. The moving water turned the wheel, and the wheel turned big stones that ground grain into flour. Later, river power ran machines that made cloth.

The land near rivers was also good for farming. When rivers flooded in spring, they left behind rich soil that helped crops grow.

Today, we can pipe water many miles and drive goods on highways. But if you look at a map, you will still see many big cities sitting beside rivers.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
