-- Passage rg4-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7fdf789a-1782-5d6b-92cd-4e0aca47f9a0', 'rg4-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Teach Typing at Oakmont School$q$, $q$Students at Oakmont School use computers almost every day. They write reports, answer questions and send messages to teachers. Yet most students type with only one or two fingers. Oakmont should teach keyboard typing starting in third grade.

Typing well saves time. A student who types with all ten fingers can be much faster than one who hunts for each key. In Ms. Adeyemi's fourth grade class, students practiced typing for ten minutes a day. By the end of the year, most of them typed twice as fast as they did in September.

Typing also helps students focus on their ideas. When students do not have to search for letters, they can think about what they want to say. Ms. Adeyemi noticed that her students wrote longer stories after they learned to type.

Many tests are now taken on computers. Students who type slowly may run out of time before they finish their answers.

Some people think students should practice handwriting instead. Handwriting is still important. But typing lessons can be short, so there is time for both.

Learning to type is a skill students will use for the rest of their lives.$q$, 'draft')
