-- Passage rg4-argument-evidence-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ee3083a3-8644-5269-922a-551473d4f825', 'rg4-argument-evidence-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Every Student Can Make Music$q$, $q$At Cedar Hill School, fourth graders can choose to join the band. Only about one in five students signs up. I think every fourth grader should learn to play an instrument.

Learning an instrument teaches patience. Nobody plays a song perfectly the first time. Students must practice the same notes again and again. Over time, they learn that hard work leads to progress.

Playing music also builds teamwork. In a band, each player has a part. If one person plays too loudly or too fast, the whole song sounds wrong. Players learn to listen to each other.

Music may even help in other subjects. Reading music means counting beats and noticing patterns, which are math skills. A music teacher named Mr. Okafor kept track of his students for three years. He found that his band students often did well on fraction lessons.

Finally, music is fun. A school concert gives students a chance to show their families what they have learned.

Some families worry about the cost of instruments. The school could keep a set of instruments for students to borrow. A neighborhood music shop also rents instruments at low prices.

Every child deserves the chance to make music.$q$, 'draft')
