insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5e3c27a3-2786-578d-a22e-be4b559da8af', 'rg4-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Hatchlings on Palm Shore$q$, $q$Account 1: A Volunteer's Notebook, by Ana Kealoha

Night of August 12. I sat on the sand next to Nest 14 with two other volunteers. We kept our flashlights off because bright lights can confuse baby turtles. Around ten o'clock, the sand on top of the nest started to sink. Then a tiny flipper popped out! Soon dozens of hatchlings were climbing over each other. They were smaller than my palm. They scrambled toward the waves, and I held my breath until the last one reached the water. My heart was pounding the whole time.

Account 2: From a Wildlife Center Fact Sheet

Each summer, volunteers watch over sea turtle nests on Palm Shore. This year, the beach had 31 nests. A mother turtle lays around one hundred eggs in each nest. The eggs hatch after about two months. Hatchlings usually come out at night, when the sand is cooler. They find the ocean by moving toward the brightest horizon, which is normally the moonlit water. Lights from houses can lead them the wrong way. That is why volunteers ask people near the beach to turn off outdoor lights.$q$, 'draft')
