-- Passage rg4-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('064323a2-28fb-55e8-b4e2-3583476ca071', 'rg4-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The Town's First Telephone$q$, $q$Account 1: A Diary Entry by Ruth Abernathy, 1902

March 3. Today Mr. Hollis put the town's first telephone on the wall of his general store. A crowd of people squeezed inside to look at the wooden box. Mr. Hollis let me turn the little crank on the side. A woman's voice came out of the earpiece and asked, "Number, please?" I was so startled that I nearly dropped it! Then Mr. Hollis talked to his brother, who lives twenty miles away in Ridgeton. I could hear a tiny, crackly voice answering him. Mother says it is a wonder, but Father says it will never replace a good letter.

Account 2: From a Sign at the Pine Hollow History Museum

Pine Hollow's first telephone was put in at Hollis General Store in 1902. Early telephones did not have dials or buttons. A caller turned a crank, which signaled an operator at a central office. The operator then connected the call by plugging wires into a large board. At first, only the store and a few businesses had telephones. By 1915, more than 200 homes in Pine Hollow had one. Many people kept writing letters, too, because calls to faraway towns cost a lot of money.$q$, 'draft')
