-- Passage rg4-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f620bbae-f9c6-5c53-bd40-72fd4f3b3070', 'rg4-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$From Lump to Bowl$q$, $q$At the community art center, potter Amara Okafor teaches students how to turn a lump of wet clay into a bowl. Each step has a reason.

First, students wedge the clay. Wedging means pressing and folding the clay on a table, a bit like kneading bread. This pushes out tiny bubbles of air. If air stays trapped inside, it can expand in the hot kiln and crack the pot.

Next, students shape the bowl on a spinning wheel. They keep their hands wet so the clay slides smoothly instead of sticking and tearing.

Then the bowl must dry slowly for about a week. Amara covers new pieces loosely with plastic. If a bowl dries too fast, the thin rim shrinks before the thick bottom does, and the bowl can crack.

After it dries, the bowl goes into a kiln, which is a very hot oven. The first firing turns the soft, crumbly clay into hard pottery. Next, students brush on glaze, a coating made of minerals. In the second firing, the glaze melts into a smooth, glassy layer. This layer makes the bowl shiny and helps it hold water.

Amara tells her students that skipping a step almost always shows up later. A crack today often started with a mistake made days before.$q$, 'draft')
